ip in hate groups usually <ei23 declines> during periods of economic prosperity . We in fact have <ei24 seen> hate group numbers <ei25 dropping> through the nineties , uh but this year they <ei28 jumped> up uh twenty percent , quite a dramatic <ei29 rise> . Mark Potach <ei30 says> over four hundred hate groups have been <ei31 identified> and many are <ei32 becoming> increasingly <ei33 successful> at recruiting younger members through the <ei34 use> of Internet web pages and the promotion of racist rock music . The Internet has certainly <ei35 helped> uh hate mongers to <ei36 organize> and uh to <ei37 feel> that they are part of a larger community , even if uh they are essentially uh , you know , angry people sitting in their bedrooms uh all alone . Uh , in addition , white power racist music has <ei38 become> uh quite popular in certain sectors . Uh , there are over fifty thousand CDs a year <ei40 sold> with uh lyrics that are quite uh unbelievable . Many of these new members are <ei41 joining> chapters of the so called Christian Identity Movement which cites Biblical scripture as a foundation for a belief in white supremacy and anti-semitism . Mark Potach <ei42 says> such members are also intensely <ei43 attracted> to apocalyptical fantasies . The <ei44 approach> of the year two thousand has many of these groups in a kind of millennial frenzy . Uh , uh a - they <ei46 believe> , or at least many of them <ei47 believe> , uh that they are <ei48 approaching> the Battle of Armageddon in which they will be pitted against the uh kind of Satanic forces <ei49 trying> to <ei50 take> over the world . Mr. Potach <ei51 notes> older , more traditional groups like the Ku Klux Klan are also <ei52 opening> new chapters , thanks in part to their <ei54 ability> to <ei55 use> new technologies like the Internet . The Federal Bureau of Investigation <ei56 says> it <ei57 received> more than eight thousand <ei58 reports> of hate group <ei59 crimes> last year . Law enforcement authorities 